Retracting — root cause found and it is not in the nightly range. Both "bad" sweeps were disturbed by a **second client using this engine concurrently** (LAN agent traffic, 50k-200k-token prompts with max_tokens in the 100k-200k range; the benchmark always sends max_tokens=128, so the requests are distinguishable in the access log). A 50-200k prefill chunked at 4096 tokens produces exactly the observed signature: 0.5-0.75 s inter-token latencies (27 in a 15 s window vs 0 in good runs), a 33k-token request preempted during a concurrency-1 cell, `vllm:num_requests_running` = 2 during c1 cells. Correlation is clean: both collapsed sweeps had a foreign request complete inside the sick cell (09:42:01 / 09:58:58 UTC); the clean isolated/prefix-replay runs had foreign traffic that missed that cell; a reduced sweep today on `dc36fcce` with zero foreign requests gives `ctx_tg @ d32768 (c1) = 155.15 ± 6.01` — no flake. My "ruled out: host interference" checked container/kernel logs but not the engine access log. My suspect list (#56512, then #56682) was wrong; the qwen4_exp ngram module is byte-identical across the range.

@ZJY0516 your reply was correct and my re-scoping after it was still noise — sorry for the churn.

One real finding survives the code-range analysis, unrelated to the flake: **#56654 reverted 6 of #56323's 28 files**, so net against pre-#56323 the V2 runner no longer pre-warms the sampler/rejection Triton kernels — the build logs `JIT kernel warmup finished in 0.00s` and vLLM's "Triton kernel JIT compilation during inference" warnings on the first post-boot requests (a few seconds of first-request latency, nothing later). Might merit a follow-up on the #56654 side.
